    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 4\n    n_machines = 4\n    processing_times = [[12, 23, 14, 30], [25, 26, 13, 29], [17, 11, 20, 26], [19, 24, 28, 24]]\n    machine_assignments = [[1, 0, 3, 2], [1, 0, 2, 3], [2, 3, 1, 0], [3, 1, 2, 0]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Plant Leadership, Production Planning, and Shop Floor Supervisors\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Job shop scheduling challenge across specialized workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized machines (workstations). We have a set of production jobs ready for release. Each job consists of a series of operations that must be executed in a fixed order across different machines. Each operation has a designated machine and a known processing duration.\n\nScheduling challenge\nWe need to determine the sequence and start times of all operations across all machines so that we respect every job\u2019s routing while making effective use of machine capacity. Because several jobs compete for the same machines at different points in their sequences, the order chosen on each machine creates waiting times and can either relieve or amplify bottlenecks. The combined effect of these choices determines how quickly we can complete all jobs.\n\nOperating rules\n- Each job follows a fixed, predefined sequence of operations.\n- Each operation is assigned to a specific machine and has a known processing time.\n- A machine can process only one operation at a time.\n- No preemption: once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption.\n- Within each job, operations must be completed in order; a downstream operation cannot begin until its predecessor finishes.\n\nGoal\nProduce a feasible, machine-by-machine schedule that sequences every operation and sets start/finish times so that all jobs are completed in the shortest possible total elapsed time (minimize makespan), while fully complying with the rules above. A strong schedule will reduce waiting and idle time, avoid unnecessary machine conflicts, and keep flow balanced across workstations.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 4\n- Number of machines (workstations): 4\n- Machine assignments by job and operation (fixed sequence): Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 3\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 3, Op4: Machine 2]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 2, Op4: Machine 3]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 3, Op3: Machine 1, Op4: Machine 0]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 3, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2, Op4: Machine 0]\n- Processing times by job and operation (aligned to the same sequence): Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 4 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 12, Op2: 23, Op3: 14, Op4: 30]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 25, Op2: 26, Op3: 13, Op4: 29]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 17, Op2: 11, Op3: 20, Op4: 26]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 19, Op2: 24, Op3: 28, Op4: 24]",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Plant Leadership, Production Planning, and Shop Floor Supervisors\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Job shop scheduling challenge across specialized workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized machines (workstations). We have a set of production jobs ready for release. Each job consists of a series of operations that must be executed in a fixed order across different machines. Each operation has a designated machine and a known processing duration.\n\nScheduling challenge\nWe need to determine the sequence and start times of all operations across all machines so that we respect every job\u2019s routing while making effective use of machine capacity. Because several jobs compete for the same machines at different points in their sequences, the order chosen on each machine creates waiting times and can either relieve or amplify bottlenecks. The combined effect of these choices determines how quickly we can complete all jobs.\n\nOperating rules\n- Each job follows a fixed, predefined sequence of operations.\n- Each operation is assigned to a specific machine and has a known processing time.\n- A machine can process only one operation at a time.\n- No preemption: once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption.\n- Within each job, operations must be completed in order; a downstream operation cannot begin until its predecessor finishes.\n\nGoal\nProduce a feasible, machine-by-machine schedule that sequences every operation and sets start/finish times so that all jobs are completed in the shortest possible total elapsed time (minimize makespan), while fully complying with the rules above. A strong schedule will reduce waiting and idle time, avoid unnecessary machine conflicts, and keep flow balanced across workstations.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Machine assignments by job and operation (fixed sequence): {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}\n- Processing times by job and operation (aligned to the same sequence): {PROCESSING_TIMES}"
